export const MYERS_AGREEMENT_MARKDOWN = "# WEBSITE DEVELOPMENT & INDEPENDENT SERVICES AGREEMENT\n\n**Myers Diesel Repair — 60-Page Website, Brand Modernization & Facility Media Production**\n\n**VERSION 1.4 — NOVEMBER 13, 2026 — DRAFT**\n\n**Complete text of the Agreement, Sections 1–24 and Schedules A–D. Not executed.**\n\n---\n\n## Agreement Summary\n\n**Service Provider:** Catalyst Digital Solutions, LLC  \n**Client:** Myers Diesel Repair, Inc.  \n**Project:** Complete Website & Digital Foundation  \n**Total Website Pages:** 60  \n**Total Project Investment:** $25,000 USD\n\n| Project Phase | Deliverables | Investment |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Phase 1 | 34 priority webpages and initial website launch | $17,000 |\n| Phase 2 | 16 additional webpages + 10 bonus webpages | $8,000 |\n| Total | 60 custom webpages | $25,000 |\n\nThe 10 bonus webpages are included at no additional charge and are binding project deliverables.\n\nThis Agreement does not authorize recurring marketing charges, advertising spending, or performance-based advertising commissions. Optional $199/month hosting and care is separately elected in Schedule D and is not part of the $25,000 fixed build price.\n\nTo be confirmed before signing\n\nBusiness identities have been prefilled, and Kenneth Wayne Myers is designated as the proposed authorized signer. The following details require confirmation before the agreement is executed:\n\n- Confirm current California Secretary of State status for Myers Diesel Repair, Inc. (entity C2734165), the legal business address, and Kenneth Wayne Myers's authority as President to sign — *Myers*\n- Confirm that **myersdiesel@icloud.com** (Ken) and **abelz@myersdiesel.com** (Abel) are the addresses the Parties authorize for formal notices — *Myers*\n- Confirm that Myers elects or declines the optional **$199/month Managed Hosting & Care Plan** in Schedule D; configure Catalyst-managed Vercel hosting and initial off-platform backups before launch — *Both Parties*\n- Optional counsel review is strongly advised before execution, particularly the cancellation and hosting terms — Both Parties\n\n## Items for confirmation before signing\n\nBusiness identities have been prefilled, and Kenneth Wayne Myers is designated as the proposed authorized signer. The following details require confirmation before the agreement is executed:\n\n- Confirm current California Secretary of State status for Myers Diesel Repair, Inc. (entity C2734165), the legal business address, and Kenneth Wayne Myers's authority as President to sign — *Myers*\n- Confirm that **myersdiesel@icloud.com** (Ken) and **abelz@myersdiesel.com** (Abel) are the addresses the Parties authorize for formal notices — *Myers*\n- Confirm that Myers elects or declines the optional **$199/month Managed Hosting & Care Plan** in Schedule D; configure Catalyst-managed Vercel hosting and initial off-platform backups before launch — *Both Parties*\n- Optional counsel review is strongly advised before execution, particularly the cancellation and hosting terms — Both Parties\n\n---\n\n## 1. Parties and Effective Date\n\nThis Website Development & Independent Services Agreement (\"Agreement\") is entered into between:\n\nService Provider (\"Catalyst\")\n\nCatalyst Digital Solutions, LLC\n\nA California Limited Liability Company\n\nRepresented by Mario Garza, Authorized Representative\n\nEmail: info@catalystdigitalsolutions.com\n\nPhone: (661) 888-4837\n\nBusiness Mailing Address: 4012 Foxwood St, Bakersfield, CA 93306\n\nClient (\"Myers\")\n\nMyers Diesel Repair, Inc.\n\nDoing business as Myers Diesel Repair\n\n9606 Enos Lane\n\nBakersfield, California 93314\n\nAuthorized Representative: Kenneth Wayne Myers, President\n\nNotice email — Ken Myers: myersdiesel@icloud.com\n\nNotice email — Abel Zazueta, Assistant CEO and CFO: abelz@myersdiesel.com\n\nCatalyst and Myers are individually a \"Party\" and collectively the \"Parties.\"\n\nThe Effective Date is the date the Agreement has been signed by both authorized representatives.\n\nThe project will commence following execution of the Agreement, receipt of the initial kickoff payment, and provision of the essential access and information required to begin work.\n\n## 2. Purpose and Objectives\n\nMyers has retained Catalyst to create a modern digital presence that communicates the business's experience, facilities, services, equipment, capabilities, and competitive advantages.\n\nThe project is designed to:\n\n- Represent the size and capabilities of Myers Diesel Repair accurately.\n- Make important repair, inspection, parts, and commercial services easier to discover and understand.\n- Give stranded drivers clear ways to request emergency assistance.\n- Help commercial fleet operators evaluate Myers as a service provider.\n- Improve website usability, mobile performance, navigation, and customer contact pathways.\n- Establish a technically sound foundation for future search optimization and advertising.\n- Present real people, equipment, facilities, and processes through professional photography and video.\n- Support future integrations and business-growth initiatives where appropriate.\n\nThe Parties acknowledge that website design and development create the foundation for future marketing but do not independently guarantee rankings, customers, or revenue.\n\n## 3. Independent Contractor and Business Relationship\n\nCatalyst is an independent business engaged to provide defined professional services.\n\nThis Agreement does not create an employer-employee relationship, agency relationship, partnership, joint venture, or corporate officer appointment.\n\nCatalyst is not an employee of Myers Diesel Repair.\n\n### 3.1 Independence and professional discretion\n\nCatalyst retains control over the professional methods, tools, processes, personnel, work location, and ordinary working schedule used to perform the contracted services, subject to agreed deadlines, deliverables, site access, and reasonable project coordination.\n\nCatalyst is responsible for its own business expenses, applicable taxes, ordinary operating costs, personnel, and professional obligations except as otherwise expressly provided.\n\nCatalyst may perform services for other businesses and maintain an independent client base, subject to this Agreement's confidentiality and intellectual-property provisions.\n\nMyers retains full control of its repair operations, personnel, business decisions, customer relationships, service policies, and business expenditures.\n\n### 3.2 Client oversight and approvals\n\nMyers has the right to:\n\n- Establish the project's business objectives.\n- Approve business-specific content and factual claims.\n- Review work against agreed requirements.\n- Request revisions within the contracted scope.\n- Approve major project changes and expenditures.\n- Hold Catalyst accountable for agreed milestones and deliverables.\n\nThese rights do not create an employment relationship or authorize Myers personnel to supervise Catalyst as an employee.\n\n### 3.3 No employment benefits or authority to bind\n\nCatalyst is not entitled to employee benefits, paid leave, overtime, payroll treatment, or other employment benefits from Myers.\n\nNeither Party may enter contracts, incur expenses, or otherwise create obligations on behalf of the other without prior written authorization.\n\nBoth Parties intend that their actual working relationship remain consistent with the independent business-to-business arrangement described here and applicable law.\n\n## 4. Project Scope\n\nCatalyst will design, develop, and deliver a custom website for Myers Diesel Repair using Next.js/React or a mutually approved modern technology stack with equivalent capabilities.\n\nThe website will use the existing primary domain: **myersdiesel.com**\n\nThe contracted project includes:\n\n1. Business and service discovery.\n2. Digital baseline research and documentation.\n3. A comprehensive 60-page website program.\n4. Custom responsive website design.\n5. Development, staging, testing, and deployment.\n6. Service-specific website copy and supporting content.\n7. Search-friendly technical foundations.\n8. Customer contact and conversion pathways.\n9. Brand modernization and approved digital graphics.\n10. Facility photography, aerial footage, and edited video deliverables.\n11. Foundational analytics and conversion-event configuration.\n12. Website launch, training, and agreed handoff.\n\nThe 60-page inventory is defined in Schedule A.\n\n### 4.1 Definition of a webpage\n\nA contracted webpage is a distinct, substantive, approved public website destination serving a meaningful customer, service, company, or resource purpose.\n\nThe Parties will prioritize accuracy, usefulness, and the commercial relevance of each page.\n\nA webpage is not defined by a minimum or maximum word count.\n\nMinor visual variations, duplicate templates displaying substantially identical content, redirects, search-result filters, and automatically generated combinations of city/service keywords do not count as separate substantive pages.\n\nStandard technical utility pages and system states, such as a 404 page or form confirmation, do not count toward the 60 substantive webpages.\n\nA future blog article is not automatically a contracted website page unless expressly designated in the approved inventory.\n\n### 4.2 Service verification\n\nMyers will confirm the services, capabilities, certifications, equipment, availability, and customer promises presented on the website.\n\nCatalyst will not knowingly publish unsupported service descriptions, certifications, inspection authorizations, or guarantees.\n\nIf a proposed service does not qualify for a dedicated page, the Parties may substitute another meaningful page under the same project scope.\n\n### 4.3 Project changes\n\nMaterial changes in page complexity, required functionality, language support, integrations, or production work require a mutually approved written change order.\n\n## 5. Phase 1 — Initial Website and Priority Pages\n\nTotal Pages\n\n34\n\nInvestment\n\n$17,000\n\nEstimated Timeline\n\nApproximately 8 weeks\n\nPhase 1 begins when the commencement conditions in Section 1 are satisfied.\n\n### Phase 1 deliverables\n\n- Initial discovery sessions with designated Myers personnel.\n- Facility, service, customer, and capability inventory.\n- Review of existing website assets and available business information.\n- Measurement of the existing digital baseline using available authorized tools.\n- Information architecture and navigation.\n- Custom website design system.\n- Responsive desktop, tablet, and mobile implementation.\n- Thirty-four approved priority webpages listed in Schedule A.\n- Core contact and emergency-service pathways.\n- Foundational technical SEO and analytics configuration.\n- Approved branding and media assets required for initial launch.\n- Staging environment.\n- Functional and responsive testing.\n- Initial production deployment.\n\n### Baseline review\n\nWhere access and data are available, Catalyst will document appropriate starting measurements, potentially including Google Search Console, Google Analytics, Google Business Profile performance, relevant search visibility, site performance, and conversion functionality.\n\nThe baseline will be used to inform future decisions and provide context for subsequent performance comparisons.\n\nNo particular baseline measurement is guaranteed where the relevant third-party data or access is unavailable.\n\n## 6. Phase 2 — Website Expansion and Bonus Pages\n\nTotal Pages\n\n26\n\nInvestment\n\n$8,000\n\nEstimated Timeline\n\nApproximately 6–8 additional weeks\n\nPhase 2 consists of:\n\n- Sixteen additional webpages from the original proposed inventory.\n- Ten additional bonus webpages at no additional charge.\n\nThe bonus pages are part of the fixed-price project and are not an optional offer.\n\n### 6.1 Ten bonus webpages\n\nCatalyst agrees to design, develop, and deliver 10 additional substantive webpages, bringing the total website program from 50 to 60 pages.\n\nNo additional development fee applies to these 10 bonus pages.\n\nThe bonus pages:\n\n- Are included in the $25,000 total project price.\n- Are assigned to Phase 2.\n- Must be meaningful, distinct pages.\n- Must relate to legitimate Myers services, customers, capabilities, equipment, or informational needs.\n- Will follow the approved website design system.\n- Will receive the same ordinary responsive-design and technical-quality treatment as comparable contracted service pages.\n- Will not be conditional on purchasing SEO, advertising, or any other monthly service.\n\nThe bonus does not include 10 additional applications, custom software integrations, ecommerce modules, or other substantially different technical products.\n\n### 6.2 Bonus-page selection\n\nThe exact topics for bonus pages 51–60 will be selected through consultation with Myers.\n\nCatalyst will consider:\n\n- Genuine customer search demand.\n- Business priorities identified by Ken, Abel, and Jeff.\n- High-volume and high-value repair opportunities.\n- Actual service profitability and operational capacity, as communicated by Myers.\n- Inspections, parts, fleet work, and specialty capabilities.\n- Opportunities to demonstrate equipment and industry expertise.\n- Gaps discovered during website research.\n\nCatalyst will propose the 10 bonus-page titles and their intended purposes in writing before Phase 2 production begins.\n\nMyers will review and approve those titles or request reasonable substitutes.\n\nThe finalized titles will be documented in a written project-scope confirmation that becomes part of Schedule A without increasing the price.\n\nThe absence of finalized bonus-page titles at signing does not eliminate Catalyst's obligation to deliver the 10 pages.\n\nIf the Parties cannot agree upon a proposed topic, they will work in good faith to identify a commercially relevant alternative of comparable complexity.\n\n### 6.3 Phase 2 completion\n\nPhase 2 is complete when all 26 additional pages have been delivered, reviewed, and accepted under this Agreement.\n\nThe complete project therefore consists of **60 substantive webpages**.\n\n## 7. Estimated Project Schedule\n\nThe proposed schedule is:\n\n| Period | Primary Activities |\n| --- | --- |\n| Weeks 1–2 | Discovery, research, facility documentation, architecture and initial design |\n| Weeks 3–5 | Design implementation, priority page production, branding and media editing |\n| Weeks 6–8 | Complete Phase 1 pages, test, review and launch |\n| Weeks 9–16 | Produce, review and publish the 26 Phase 2 pages |\n\nThe estimated full-project duration is approximately 14–16 weeks from commencement.\n\nPhase planning may overlap when practical.\n\nThese dates are reasonable estimates, not unconditional delivery guarantees.\n\nSchedules depend on timely access, customer approvals, content verification, filming conditions, and reasonable technical readiness.\n\nCatalyst will notify Myers of material schedule changes.\n\nWhere material client information or approvals remain outstanding for more than 10 business days after written request, Catalyst may pause affected work and propose revised milestones.\n\n## 8. Project Investment and Payment Terms\n\nThe complete fixed project investment is:\n\n$25,000 USD\n\nThis includes all 60 contracted webpages and the branding and media deliverables expressly described in this Agreement.\n\nThe 10 bonus webpages do not increase the project price.\n\n### 8.1 Payment schedule\n\n| Payment | Amount | Timing |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Phase 1 kickoff | $8,500 | Upon execution, before work begins |\n| Phase 1 completion | $8,500 | Upon Phase 1 staging acceptance, before initial launch |\n| Phase 2 kickoff | $4,000 | Before Phase 2 production begins |\n| Phase 2 completion | $4,000 | Upon Phase 2 staging acceptance, before final publication |\n| Total | $25,000 |  |\n\nEach payment is part of the agreed $25,000 project price.\n\nThe initial $8,500 payment is not an additional setup fee.\n\n### 8.2 Payment methods\n\nCatalyst will provide an invoice or authorized secure payment link. Payments may be processed through Stripe or another agreed method. No milestone other than an amount separately authorized by Myers will be automatically charged.\n\nThe $25,000 website fee is not a subscription. Recurring **Managed Hosting & Care** charges of $199/month are permitted only if Myers affirmatively selects and signs the optional service election in Schedule D or a later separate written service order. The initial $8,500 kickoff checkout does not itself enroll Myers in recurring billing. Advertising, SEO/content, and performance-commission services always require separate express authorization.\n\n### 8.3 Late payment\n\nIf an undisputed milestone payment is overdue, Catalyst will provide written notice.\n\nIf the undisputed amount remains unpaid five business days after notice, Catalyst may suspend further work or delay publication of the affected phase until payment is resolved.\n\nThe Parties will attempt in good faith to resolve genuine billing disputes.\n\n### 8.4 Separate services and expenses\n\nThe fixed development price does not authorize recurring SEO/content management, advertising services, media spending, or performance commissions. Managed hosting and care are addressed only in Section 17 and the optional Schedule D election.\n\nAny legally required applicable taxes and separately authorized external services will be disclosed and documented. No unapproved third-party cost may be charged to Myers.\n\n## 9. Brand Modernization and Digital Graphics\n\nThe project includes a defined Myers Diesel brand-modernization package.\n\n### Included deliverables\n\n**Logo and wordmark**\n\n- One selected modernized vector-logo direction.\n- Refinement or recreation of the existing Myers Diesel wordmark in appropriate vector form.\n- Up to three approved wordmark/logo lockups, where useful and visually feasible.\n- Practical web-ready and vector export formats.\n\n**Brand guide**\n\nA concise brand guide covering:\n\n- Primary and secondary colors.\n- Approved typography.\n- Logo and wordmark usage.\n- Basic spacing and clear-space recommendations.\n- Light and dark background variations where appropriate.\n- Basic digital brand consistency.\n\n**Website and social graphics**\n\n- Website favicon.\n- One Open Graph link-preview image.\n- Social avatars for the agreed platforms.\n- Platform-appropriate banners and profile-header graphics for Facebook, LinkedIn, X, and other approved platforms where supported.\n- Appropriate Instagram and TikTok profile identity assets.\n- Google Business Profile cover/identity graphics and selected image crops.\n\n### Revision allowance\n\nThe brand work includes up to two initial creative directions and two consolidated revision rounds for the selected direction.\n\nMajor additional concepts, repeated reversals of approved directions, or new branding campaigns require a written change order.\n\n### Exclusions\n\nUnless separately agreed, the project does not include:\n\n- Trademark registration or legal trademark clearance.\n- Embroidery digitization.\n- Vehicle-wrap production or installation.\n- Large-scale printed collateral.\n- Sign manufacturing.\n- Unlimited promotional graphics.\n- Extensive additional identity campaigns.\n\n## 10. Facility Photography, Drone Video, and Media Production\n\nThe project includes an on-site media-production package intended to bring the Myers Diesel in-person facility experience online.\n\nThe goal is to show the actual operation, people, facilities, equipment, capabilities, and processes.\n\n### 10.1 Included production time\n\nCatalyst will provide or coordinate:\n\n- One scheduled on-site production session of up to eight hours.\n- One follow-up or pickup session of up to three hours if reasonably needed.\n- Ground-level photography.\n- Professional video capture.\n- Aerial/drone capture where lawful and operationally safe.\n- Basic production planning and shot selection.\n\nCatalyst may perform production directly or use qualified subcontractors at Catalyst's discretion.\n\nOrdinary production and subcontractor expenses necessary to fulfill the agreed allowance are included in the fixed project price.\n\n### 10.2 Included finished media\n\nThe finished deliverables include:\n\n**Photography — 30 final photographs.** Catalyst will deliver **30 professionally edited, web-ready photographs** for the website. Catalyst will capture at least 20 original photographs during the included on-site sessions and may select and professionally edit up to 10 existing Myers-supplied photographs (if appropriate source quality and usage rights are confirmed). If fewer than 10 Myers-supplied photographs are suitable, Catalyst will capture or produce sufficient additional original photography within the scheduled production allowance to deliver 30 final photographs. Myers will provide reasonable site access and any existing photographs it wishes considered. No AI-generated or third-party stock image will be substituted for a photograph of the actual Myers facility, staff, or equipment without express written approval.\n\n**Main facility video.** One edited primary facility-tour video, approximately 45–60 seconds in length.\n\n**Website video segments.** Four edited short video segments, approximately 10–30 seconds each.\n\nThe short videos may highlight themes such as roadside assistance, commercial repair facilities, heavy equipment, parts operations, and quality-control practices.\n\nFinal creative selections depend on footage, business priorities, and available filming opportunities.\n\n### 10.3 Media treatment\n\nIncluded editing may involve:\n\n- Color and exposure correction.\n- Reasonable cropping and reframing.\n- Appropriate stabilization.\n- Video sequencing and transitions.\n- Basic titles or approved brand overlays.\n- Compression and optimization for website delivery.\n- Suitable final exports.\n\nComplex animation, extensive visual effects, additional narration production, elaborate sound design, or major documentary production are not included unless separately approved.\n\n### 10.4 Safety and operating conditions\n\nMyers will coordinate access to its premises and identify restricted areas, active hazards, confidential displays, and appropriate filming windows.\n\nCatalyst and any production subcontractor will comply with applicable site-safety requirements.\n\nDrone operations are subject to applicable FAA rules, operator qualifications, aircraft registration, Remote ID requirements, airspace authorization, weather, and safe operating conditions.\n\nCatalyst will not knowingly perform unauthorized or unsafe drone operations.\n\nIf aerial capture cannot lawfully or safely occur, the Parties will agree in writing upon reasonable substitute footage, rescheduling, or an appropriate adjustment to the affected media deliverable.\n\n### 10.5 Permissions and releases\n\nMyers will help coordinate employee and site permissions where required.\n\nCatalyst will obtain appropriate usage rights for any subcontracted production, stock materials, licensed music, or other third-party materials it supplies.\n\nThe Parties will cooperate to avoid publishing sensitive customer information, confidential computer displays, protected records, or identifiable persons without appropriate permission.\n\n### 10.6 Finished deliverables versus raw footage\n\nThe contracted media package includes the finished approved photographs and edited videos described above.\n\nUnused footage, unedited camera files, working project files, and unused production concepts are not automatically included in the handoff unless otherwise agreed in writing.\n\nAdditional shoot days or finished media beyond the contracted allowance require a written change order.\n\n## 11. Website Design and Development Requirements\n\nCatalyst will implement a custom responsive website intended to provide a professional and accessible user experience.\n\nThe included work covers the following areas where appropriate to the approved design.\n\n### Navigation and presentation\n\n- Custom homepage.\n- Clear service categorization.\n- Desktop and mobile navigation.\n- Meaningful service-page organization.\n- Actual company imagery.\n- Facility and equipment presentation.\n- Clear commercial and customer contact pathways.\n\n### Customer conversion\n\n- Prominent tap-to-call functionality.\n- Emergency contact calls to action on relevant pages.\n- Fleet and parts inquiry pathways.\n- Appropriate contact forms.\n- Clear next-step messaging.\n- Mobile usability and legible content.\n\nAll published service hours and availability claims require Myers' approval.\n\n### Technical SEO foundation\n\n- Logical page URLs.\n- Relevant page titles and descriptions.\n- Canonical URL configuration where appropriate.\n- XML sitemap.\n- Robots directives.\n- Suitable structured data.\n- Internal linking.\n- Preservation or redirection of relevant legacy URLs where practical.\n- Appropriate indexing configuration.\n- Basic performance optimization.\n\n### Analytics foundation\n\nSubject to authorized account access:\n\n- Google Analytics configuration or integration.\n- Google Search Console verification.\n- Basic contact-event and conversion tracking.\n- Form submission measurements where supported.\n- Telephone-link interaction tracking where technically appropriate.\n- Basic functional checks before launch.\n\nActual lead-to-repair-order and invoice-level revenue attribution is not included in the website-development scope unless separately agreed.\n\n### Development and launch\n\n- Private staging or preview environment.\n- Reasonable desktop and mobile testing.\n- Production deployment.\n- Domain configuration.\n- Post-launch functional checks.\n- Basic client handoff and orientation.\n\nCatalyst may use Supabase or another suitable service to organize verified company information and support the website's content architecture.\n\nThis does not authorize replacement or integration of Myers' proprietary repair-management software.\n\n## 12. Client Participation and Responsibilities\n\nMyers agrees to provide reasonable cooperation, including:\n\n- Access to relevant business and technical information.\n- Appropriate domain and website credentials.\n- Authorized access to analytics and business profiles as needed.\n- Verification of service descriptions and capabilities.\n- Review of inspection and regulatory claims.\n- Availability of designated personnel for necessary discovery.\n- Reasonable facility access for photography and video.\n- Timely feedback and approvals.\n- Confirmation of approved customer contact methods.\n- Approved milestone payments.\n\nMyers will designate one primary representative to coordinate feedback and ordinary approvals.\n\nKen Myers may provide overall business direction and major approvals.\n\nAbel Zazueta, Assistant CEO and CFO, may coordinate operational, financial, and technical matters as authorized by Myers.\n\nTheir exact approval authority will be identified by the Client in writing.\n\nMyers retains responsibility for its business operations, professional qualifications, regulatory compliance, customer promises, warranties, and the accuracy of information supplied or approved for publication.\n\n## 13. Communication and Project Accountability\n\nCatalyst will provide reasonable updates at project milestones and communicate material delays or issues promptly.\n\nDepending on the work being reviewed, updates may include:\n\n- Written summaries.\n- Website preview links.\n- Screenshots or annotated comparisons.\n- Recorded walkthroughs.\n- Review meetings.\n- Milestone completion notices.\n\nThe objective is to allow Myers management to monitor meaningful progress without becoming involved in Catalyst's daily production processes.\n\nCatalyst is responsible for performing contracted services in a professional manner and meeting agreed deliverable requirements.\n\nMyers is responsible for providing business decisions, feedback, and approvals within the agreed process.\n\nNo provision requires Catalyst to attend daily employee meetings, maintain fixed employee-style working hours, or remain continuously available as a member of Myers' staff.\n\n## 14. Mutual Professional Conduct\n\nThe Parties agree that productive business relationships require professional communication, mutual respect, and clear accountability.\n\nNeither Party is required to tolerate:\n\n- Threats or intimidation.\n- Harassment.\n- Discriminatory abuse.\n- Repeated personally abusive communications.\n- Conduct intended to undermine the other Party through humiliation or coercion.\n\nOrdinary disagreements over quality, fees, scope, priorities, deadlines, or business decisions do not constitute prohibited conduct merely because they are strongly expressed.\n\nConcerns should first be raised through the designated principals when doing so is reasonable and safe.\n\nFor less serious or correctable conduct issues, the Parties will attempt to resolve the issue promptly and in good faith.\n\nSevere misconduct or repeated abusive conduct following written notice may justify suspension of direct interaction or termination under Section 21, subject to applicable law.\n\nThis clause applies equally to both Parties.\n\nIt does not waive accrued payment obligations or allow either Party to avoid legitimate contractual responsibilities.\n\n## 15. Review, Revisions, and Acceptance\n\nCatalyst will provide a staging version of each phase for review.\n\n### Review period\n\nMyers will have seven business days after receiving access and written notice of readiness to:\n\n1. Approve the submitted phase in writing; or\n2. Provide one consolidated written list of material deficiencies against the contracted scope.\n\nCatalyst will reasonably correct in-scope material deficiencies and resubmit the affected work.\n\n### Included revisions\n\nThe project includes up to two consolidated revision rounds for each major design stage, along with corrections of demonstrable defects in agreed functionality.\n\nA revision round is one reasonably organized set of comments submitted through the designated approval contact.\n\nSubstantial new ideas, additional features, previously rejected design directions, or changes to approved business strategy are subject to a change order.\n\n### Failure to respond\n\nIf Myers does not respond within the review period, Catalyst will provide a written reminder.\n\nIf no response or material deficiency notice is received within five additional business days, the submitted phase may be treated as accepted for billing and scheduling purposes, provided the contracted deliverables have been made available for reasonable review.\n\nDeemed acceptance does not eliminate the limited defect warranty.\n\n### Publication\n\nPhase 1 will be published following acceptance, payment of the Phase 1 completion milestone, and technical readiness.\n\nPhase 2 pages will be published following acceptance and payment of the Phase 2 completion milestone.\n\n## 16. Website Ownership, Intellectual Property, and Handoff\n\n### 16.1 Client materials\n\nMyers retains ownership of its pre-existing business names, logos, trademarks, customer records, original content, photographs, and other materials supplied to Catalyst.\n\nMyers represents that it has the rights necessary to authorize Catalyst's use of materials it supplies.\n\n### 16.2 Custom project deliverables\n\nUpon full payment for the applicable accepted phase, Catalyst assigns to Myers its transferable intellectual-property rights in the final, original, project-specific deliverables created for that phase, excluding the retained materials described below.\n\nThese may include:\n\n- Approved custom website designs.\n- Project-specific website code.\n- Original project-specific copy.\n- Finished approved brand graphics.\n- Final edited facility photographs and videos, to the extent Catalyst owns transferable rights in them.\n- Other expressly contracted final assets.\n\n### 16.3 Reusable technology\n\nCatalyst retains ownership of pre-existing and independently developed:\n\n- Software tools.\n- Code libraries.\n- Reusable website components.\n- Templates and frameworks.\n- Design systems.\n- AI and automation tools.\n- Content-generation systems.\n- Development methods.\n- Internal operating processes.\n\nWhere reusable Catalyst-owned technology is embedded in the completed website, Catalyst grants Myers a perpetual, worldwide, nonexclusive, royalty-free license to use, operate, maintain, and modify that technology as part of the delivered website and its reasonable successor versions.\n\nThird-party and open-source materials remain subject to their respective licenses.\n\n### 16.4 Account ownership\n\nThe primary domain **myersdiesel.com**, its registration, and Myers-owned business accounts remain owned or controlled by Myers. With Myers's authorization, Catalyst may manage DNS and technical settings as a delegated administrator. Catalyst will not transfer domain registration to itself, alter business email/MX records without approval, or treat the domain as security for unpaid service fees.\n\nProduction hosting will initially operate within **Catalyst's managed Vercel Pro infrastructure**, with Catalyst retaining administrative access sufficient to deploy, update, troubleshoot, and secure the website. This is hosting administration, not ownership of Myers's domain or of paid-for custom website deliverables. Myers may request reasonable records or an export of its website and may leave Catalyst's hosting according to Section 17.\n\n### 16.5 Handoff\n\nAfter payment and acceptance of the relevant phase, Catalyst will provide the applicable paid-for custom project files, approved graphics, finished media, and production configuration information according to the handoff and exit procedures in Section 17. Myers is entitled to operate the paid-for website using Catalyst's licensed reusable components as specified in Section 16.3.\n\nMyers is not required to purchase Catalyst's $4,000/month search-and-content program or any advertising service. Managed hosting is an optional, separately authorized $199/month service if Myers chooses to remain on Catalyst infrastructure after the complimentary project hosting period.\n\n## 17. Third-Party Accounts and Expenses\n\n### 17.1 Hosting platform, control, and domain\n\nCatalyst will deploy the production website within **Catalyst-managed Vercel Pro infrastructure** or an equivalent commercial hosting environment approved in writing by both Parties. Catalyst will have administrative control of the hosting project, deployments, technical configuration, and associated source-control workflow so it can promptly maintain and troubleshoot the site. Catalyst will be the hosting provider of record to Myers and will pay Vercel for the included managed infrastructure.\n\n**Myers retains ownership and registrant control of myersdiesel.com** and all paid-for website deliverables under Section 16. Domain registration/renewal charges remain Myers's responsibility. Myers grants Catalyst limited permission to manage DNS and connect the domain to the site during the engagement; changes affecting email service, registrant contact, or other unrelated domain records require Myers's prior approval. Catalyst will not hold the domain or accepted paid-for content as leverage in a service dispute.\n\n### 17.2 Complimentary project hosting and start of paid care\n\nProduction hosting on Catalyst infrastructure is **included at no separate charge throughout active performance of Phases 1 and 2** and for **30 calendar days after final Phase 2 publication and acceptance** (the \"Complimentary Hosting Period\"). If the Agreement ends or is terminated before Phase 2 completion, complimentary hosting for a launched site continues for 30 calendar days after the effective termination date, subject to lawful suspension for a serious security threat or misuse.\n\nTo remain on Catalyst-managed infrastructure after that period, Myers must affirmatively elect the separate **Managed Hosting & Care Plan at $199 per month** under Schedule D or a later written service order. The fee is billed monthly in advance from the day after the Complimentary Hosting Period ends, and is not included in the fixed $25,000 build price. No recurring charge is authorized solely by signing or paying for the website build unless the Schedule D plan is separately selected and signed.\n\nIf Myers activates Catalyst's separately contracted **$4,000/month Search Everywhere & Content Program**, the $199 monthly hosting-and-care fee is included within that $4,000 while that program remains active; Catalyst will not bill both fees for the same month. No $4,000 monthly program is created by this Agreement.\n\n### 17.3 Included Managed Hosting & Care services\n\nFor each month in which the $199 care plan is active and paid (or included in an active separately contracted $4,000 program), Catalyst will provide:\n\n- Commercial Vercel hosting, standard CDN delivery, and configuration/renewal of provider-managed HTTPS/SSL certificates, subject to valid domain records.\n- Reasonable automated availability/error monitoring and investigation of detected site-wide outages; an effort to start triage within one business day after Catalyst becomes aware of a material outage, without an around-the-clock support guarantee.\n- Routine application dependency/security patch assessment and compatible maintenance deployments, within the existing website architecture; major framework migrations and redesigns are excluded.\n- Version-controlled source and content backups and the recovery procedures in Section 17.4.\n- Up to **60 minutes of minor site edits per calendar month**, including straightforward text, business-hour, phone, or image replacements requested through the agreed channel. Unused time does not accumulate; new pages, complex changes, videos, branding work, and third-party integrations are excluded.\n- Reasonable technical assistance coordinating hosting renewals, standard SSL issues, and applicable deployment or configuration updates.\n\nWork outside these limits may be proposed at **$150/hour** or under a fixed-price change order, but will not be charged without Myers's written approval. Catalyst will not separately pass through ordinary Vercel usage incurred within the agreed website architecture and normal use. Unusual paid third-party upgrades or substantial extraordinary traffic/storage costs must be discussed and approved before separate billing; any lawful provider-imposed immediate security/usage limits will be disclosed promptly.\n\n### 17.4 Backups, recovery, and data boundaries\n\nCatalyst will maintain a version-controlled copy of custom website source code and approved substantive website content. During the active managed-care period, Catalyst will maintain a **restorable, separately stored backup or export of the source and content at least weekly**, retaining the most recent four weekly copies. A restorable source/content export will also be supplied at final paid project handoff and upon termination as provided below.\n\nIf the approved implementation stores persistent database or customer-submitted data, Catalyst will document the database location and implement automated **daily backups with at least 14 days' rolling retention**, subject to suitable storage/backup infrastructure being configured before production use. If no mutable database is used, no database backup is necessary. Backups of form submissions or CRM records hosted in independent third-party systems are outside this service unless expressly documented.\n\nDeployment history, rollback, and CDN caching are not substitutes for an independent database backup. Catalyst will document the basic restoration method and perform one reasonable restore test at final project handoff. Disaster recovery depends on the scope of the outage and the availability of external providers; no fixed recovery time or restoration point is guaranteed.\n\n### 17.5 Availability, maintenance exclusions, and support limitations\n\nCatalyst will use commercially reasonable efforts to maintain the website, but does not guarantee uninterrupted service, a specific uptime percentage, 24-hour staff availability, or immunity from third-party outages, attacks, or provider changes. Vercel's plan features are supplied under Vercel's terms and do not constitute a separate Catalyst uptime SLA.\n\nThe care plan does not include unlimited content changes, new webpages, redesigns, advertising management, SEO/content production, customer-relationship software, AI agents, custom analytics dashboards, emergency after-hours technical labor, major backend upgrades, or third-party charges unless specifically stated in writing.\n\n### 17.6 Monthly term, renewals, and payment\n\nThe Managed Hosting & Care Plan, if elected, operates on a **month-to-month basis** without an annual minimum, billed in advance. Its $199 monthly fee remains fixed for the first 12 paid months; subsequent fee changes require at least 30 days' advance written notice and may be rejected by ending the service. Either Party may end the plan on at least 30 calendar days' written notice; prepaid service fees for a fully delivered monthly period are not prorated solely for a voluntary mid-month departure, subject to applicable law and any failure to provide the contracted service.\n\nIf an undisputed care invoice remains unpaid, Catalyst will provide written notice to both Ken and Abel and at least 15 calendar days to cure before suspending future care services or hosted site access. Catalyst will provide a reasonable opportunity for export or transition under Section 17.7. Nothing authorizes Catalyst to revoke Myers's domain registration or withhold paid-for site assets.\n\n### 17.7 Exit, transfer, and customer portability\n\nAt Myers's written request, or when the hosting relationship ends, Catalyst will provide within **10 business days** after payment of undisputed earned amounts, to the extent technically available: source-code repository access or export for paid-for deliverables, approved substantive content, final site assets, a description of hosting configuration, necessary DNS transition information, and an export of any relevant site-owned persistent data in a commonly usable format. Credentials will be transferred securely. Third-party licensed components remain governed by their licenses.\n\nCatalyst will reasonably cooperate with an orderly transition to a Myers-owned Vercel account or another qualified hosting provider and will provide up to **two hours of standard transition assistance without additional charge**. Additional migration/rebuild work is separately quoted and approved. A project-to-project transfer may be available on Vercel subject to provider capabilities; source export and a reasonable new deployment are alternatives when direct transfer is not feasible.\n\nMyers must maintain an alternate hosting arrangement after leaving Catalyst's service. Catalyst will keep an available export for **30 calendar days after hosting termination**, then may securely remove the hosted copies and non-required backups, subject to legally required retention and the Parties' confidentiality obligations. Catalyst is not required to provide free production hosting after the stated complimentary or paid period, but will not deliberately interfere with reasonable transition.\n\n### 17.8 Interaction with other provisions\n\nThese terms govern hosting and care and supplement Sections 8, 16, 19, 21 and Schedule D. The 30-day workmanship/defect warranty in Section 19 remains part of the build price and is not dependent on purchasing the monthly care plan. When a separately signed marketing program includes hosting and care, the program's written terms govern its scope and billing, provided Myers is not charged twice. Website hosting does not grant Catalyst ownership of Myers's domain or paid-for custom deliverables.\n\n## 18. Confidentiality and Information Security\n\nEach Party will protect nonpublic business information received from the other and use it only for authorized purposes.\n\nConfidential information may include:\n\n- Internal financial information.\n- Customer and fleet records.\n- Internal pricing.\n- Business procedures.\n- Credentials.\n- Nonpublic company information.\n- Proprietary software information.\n- Project information specifically identified as confidential.\n\nCatalyst may disclose necessary information to authorized subcontractors and service providers subject to appropriate confidentiality arrangements.\n\nCredentials should be exchanged using secure methods, with appropriate access permissions and multifactor authentication where available.\n\nCatalyst will not knowingly publish confidential customer records, internal company information, or sensitive personal data.\n\nClient approval is required before confidential repair/customer information is submitted to third-party AI systems for uses outside an already authorized and suitably protected workflow.\n\nEach Party will promptly notify the other upon discovering an actual unauthorized disclosure of project-related confidential information within its control.\n\nThese obligations survive the termination of this Agreement to the extent required by law and the continuing confidentiality of the information.\n\n## 19. Quality Warranty and Performance Limitations\n\nCatalyst will perform the contracted services professionally and in a workmanlike manner.\n\n### Limited defect warranty\n\nFor 30 calendar days following the public launch of each paid phase, Catalyst will correct reproducible material defects in its delivered work that prevent the applicable phase from meeting agreed functional requirements.\n\nThis warranty does not include:\n\n- New features.\n- New design directions.\n- Additional pages.\n- Third-party outages.\n- Client modifications causing the defect.\n- Unsupported integrations.\n- Work outside the agreed scope.\n\n### No guaranteed business outcomes\n\nCatalyst does not guarantee:\n\n- A particular Google ranking.\n- First-place placement in Maps.\n- A particular AI-search recommendation.\n- A fixed number of leads or customers.\n- Specific website traffic.\n- Advertising profitability.\n- An increase in business revenue.\n\nSearch visibility and website results depend on customer demand, competition, third-party search platforms, and operational factors outside Catalyst's exclusive control.\n\nThe Parties understand that replacing an existing website may involve temporary search visibility fluctuations even when appropriate migration precautions are followed.\n\n## 20. Change Orders and Additional Work\n\nMaterial changes outside the agreed scope require written approval from both Parties.\n\nA change order should describe:\n\n- Additional or substituted deliverables.\n- Cost.\n- Timeline impact.\n- Payment schedule.\n- Any changed acceptance criteria.\n\nCatalyst will not charge for unapproved additions.\n\nMyers will not require material additional work without a written scope change.\n\nThe 10 bonus pages are already included in this Agreement and do not require an additional fee or change order merely to select their approved topics.\n\n## 21. Suspension and Termination\n\n### 21.1 Termination for material breach\n\nEither Party may provide written notice of a material breach, describing the breach and requested cure. Except where immediate termination is lawful and reasonably necessary due to serious wrongdoing, an actual security threat, or illegal conduct, the receiving Party will have 10 business days to cure. If not cured, the nonbreaching Party may terminate in writing. Myers will not owe a cancellation penalty if it properly terminates because of Catalyst's uncured material breach. The Parties will reconcile any properly delivered and accepted services as required by applicable law.\n\n### 21.2 Cancellation for convenience and treatment of the kickoff advance\n\nEither Party may terminate the uncompleted project for convenience upon 15 business days' written notice to the official notice addresses in Section 24. The **$8,500 Phase 1 kickoff payment is an advance credited toward project services**, not a separate penalty, forfeiture, or nonrefundable charge solely because it was paid. Once work starts, Catalyst is entitled to apply that advance toward **documented professional work actually performed, properly earned milestone amounts, and previously approved noncancelable costs** under this Section. Unused portions will be returned in the reconciliation below.\n\nThe Parties intend this clause to pay Catalyst for real services and commitments—not to assess a speculative lost-profit or reserved-capacity charge for work that was never performed. Catalyst need not accept new or changed work after receiving a termination notice, but may perform reasonable, documented wind-down and transition work during the notice period where required to preserve paid-for deliverables.\n\n### 21.3 Earned milestone fees and unfinished work\n\n**Accepted milestones remain payable** at the fixed milestone price even if publication is temporarily held pending payment, provided their acceptance requirements were satisfied under Section 15. For services performed toward an unfinished, unaccepted milestone (including Phase 2 and the 10 bonus pages), Catalyst may charge **$150/hour for contemporaneously documented, in-scope work actually performed**, limited to the remaining unpaid amount of the applicable phase after crediting amounts already earned for that phase. This termination-only valuation does not turn the fixed-fee project into ordinary hourly billing.\n\nPreviously approved noncancelable third-party costs may be recovered to the extent legally permitted and not already included in an earned milestone or documented work charge. Ordinary overhead, unused production capacity, and future work not performed are not separately billable as cancellation damages. No expense or work may be billed twice.\n\n### 21.4 Itemized reconciliation and refund\n\nWithin 10 business days after termination, Catalyst will supply an itemized statement showing: (a) accepted milestone fees earned; plus (b) properly documented unfinished work at the agreed termination-only rate, within the remaining phase price; plus (c) approved noncancelable third-party commitments not otherwise included; minus (d) all payments and credits already received. Amounts due for project services cannot exceed the **$25,000 fixed project price** before any separately approved external expense. The advance kickoff amount is credited, never charged again.\n\nIf prior payments exceed earned and reimbursable amounts, Catalyst will refund the difference within 15 business days after the final reconciliation. If earned and reimbursable amounts exceed prior payments, Myers will pay the undisputed difference within 15 business days after invoice. A disputed item will not delay delivery of Client-owned domain credentials or files for undisputed paid-for deliverables. This Section replaces any percentage-based kill fee or automatic forfeiture of the kickoff advance.\n\n### 21.5 Catalyst-initiated termination and hosting continuity\n\nIf Catalyst terminates for convenience, it will refund all unearned amounts, deliver paid-for work in progress as reasonably usable, and cooperate with hosting transition under Section 17. Catalyst will not bill Myers for work that Catalyst elects not to perform. If either Party terminates while a Phase 1 site is publicly launched, the complimentary hosting/transition period is governed by Section 17.2; the month-to-month Managed Hosting & Care Plan may be separately continued or terminated under its own terms.\n\n### 21.6 No exclusion of legal remedies\n\nNothing in this Section limits remedies that applicable law requires or permits for fraud, intentional wrongdoing, or other nonwaivable claims. The Parties intend this termination reconciliation to be compensatory rather than punitive, and acknowledge that its enforceability ultimately depends on applicable law and the circumstances of performance.\n\n## 22. Liability and Allocation of Responsibilities\n\nEach Party is responsible for its own conduct, representations, and contractual obligations. Myers is responsible for its repair operations, customer relations, professional certifications, business policies, physical premises, and regulatory compliance. Catalyst is responsible for the professional website-development and media-production services expressly undertaken in this Agreement.\n\n### 22.1 Exclusion of certain damages\n\nTo the maximum extent permitted by law, neither Party will be liable to the other for indirect, special, incidental, exemplary, punitive, or consequential damages; speculative lost profits; loss of prospective business; or indirect interruption losses arising from this Agreement, even if advised of their possibility. This exclusion does not limit a Party's liability where such a limit is prohibited by law or under the express carve-outs below.\n\n### 22.2 General aggregate cap\n\nExcept for the express exceptions in Section 22.3, each Party's aggregate liability for ordinary claims arising under or relating to this Agreement will not exceed the **greater of (a) the fees actually paid or contractually payable under this Agreement in the 12 months immediately preceding the first event giving rise to the claim or (b) $8,500**, and in no event will the general cap exceed the **$25,000 fixed project fee**. All related claims arising from the same or connected events count toward one aggregate cap.\n\n### 22.3 Exclusions and carve-outs\n\nThe general monetary cap does **not** limit (i) Myers's obligation to pay undisputed, legally owed fees and approved third-party amounts; (ii) fraud, intentional wrongdoing or willful misconduct; or (iii) any liability that applicable law does not permit the Parties to exclude or limit. For ordinary negligent breaches of Section 18 (confidentiality/security) or third-party intellectual-property infringement caused by a Party's supplied or created materials, the aggregate direct-damages cap is **two times the general cap**, unless the conduct is intentionally wrongful or cannot lawfully be capped. Each Party is responsible for obtaining reasonable permissions for materials it contributes as allocated in Sections 10 and 16. Neither Party assumes an open-ended duty to indemnify or defend the other beyond liabilities expressly stated here or imposed by applicable law.\n\n### 22.4 No waiver of payments or mandatory rights\n\nThese provisions do not waive a Party's right to seek payment of earned fees, a court order protecting intellectual property or confidential information, or another remedy that cannot be lawfully restricted. Nothing requires either Party to maintain insurance not separately agreed in writing. The allocation of risk in this section is part of the negotiated fixed-price terms.\n\n## 23. Dispute Resolution and Governing Law\n\nThe Parties agree to attempt resolution of contract disputes through good-faith discussion between authorized principals.\n\nThe Party raising the dispute will provide a written statement describing the issue and requested remedy.\n\nUnless immediate relief is reasonably necessary, the Parties will allow 15 business days for good-faith resolution discussions.\n\nThis Agreement is governed by California law.\n\nSubject to applicable law, any judicial proceeding arising under this Agreement will be brought in a court of competent jurisdiction serving Kern County, California.\n\nNothing prevents either Party from seeking appropriate urgent legal relief to protect confidential information, intellectual property, or other legally protected rights.\n\n## 24. General Provisions\n\n**Entire Agreement.** This Agreement, Schedules A–C, any separately elected Schedule D, approved project-scope confirmations, and signed change orders constitute the complete agreement for the contracted website project. Earlier discussions, presentations, or proposals do not modify these terms unless expressly incorporated.\n\n**Amendments.** Material changes must be agreed upon in writing by authorized representatives.\n\n**Severability.** If a provision is held unenforceable, the remaining provisions remain in force to the extent permitted by law.\n\n**No Waiver.** Failure to enforce a provision does not waive future enforcement.\n\n**Assignment.** Neither Party may assign this Agreement in a way that materially affects the other Party's rights or obligations without reasonable prior written consent, except as permitted by law.\n\n**Notices.** Contractual notices, formal approvals, material-change notices, termination notices, and official project documents must be sent by email to **both** Kenneth Wayne Myers, President, at **myersdiesel@icloud.com**, and Abel Zazueta, Assistant CEO and CFO, at **abelz@myersdiesel.com**, with a copy to Catalyst at **info@catalystdigitalsolutions.com**. Notices to Catalyst from Myers must be sent to that Catalyst address and should copy both Myers representatives. Each Party may change its designated email by written notice to the other. Routine project correspondence may use other agreed channels, but it does not replace the required formal notice recipients. A notice is deemed delivered upon confirmation of successful transmission without a bounce-back, subject to applicable law.\n\n**Electronic Transactions.** The Parties consent to conducting this transaction electronically and to using authenticated electronic signatures and records where permitted by applicable law.\n\n**Counterparts.** The Agreement may be executed in electronic or physical counterparts that together form one agreement.\n\n**Authority.** Each signatory represents that they are authorized to bind the entity on whose behalf they sign.\n\n**Separate Payment Authorization.** Payment of an invoice or Stripe kickoff checkout does not independently authorize additional services. The optional $199/month care plan requires an affirmative Schedule D election or later written order. No SEO/content subscription, advertising expenditure, or performance-based commission is created by the website payment.\n\n## Schedule A — 60-Page Website Inventory\n\nThe following schedule identifies the contracted website architecture.\n\nPhase 1 includes 34 pages.\n\nPhase 2 includes 16 originally proposed pages plus 10 bonus pages.\n\nThe Parties may approve comparable substitutions in writing where necessary to improve business relevance or avoid repetitive content.\n\n### Category A — Core Business & Contact\n\n1. Homepage — Phase 1\n\n2. Services Overview — Phase 1\n\n3. Emergency & Roadside Assistance — Phase 1\n\n4. Fleet Maintenance & Accounts — Phase 1\n\n5. Truck Parts & Requests — Phase 1\n\n6. About Myers Diesel — Phase 1\n\n7. Contact, Directions & Departments — Phase 1\n\n### Category B — Repair Services\n\n8. Diesel Engine Repair — Phase 1\n\n9. Engine Diagnostics & Troubleshooting — Phase 1\n\n10. Engine Overhauls & Rebuilds — Phase 2\n\n11. Transmission & Clutch Repair — Phase 1\n\n12. Driveline Repair — Phase 1\n\n13. Custom Driveline Fabrication — Phase 2, subject to service verification\n\n14. Differentials & Axles — Phase 2\n\n15. Heavy-Duty Suspension Repair — Phase 1\n\n16. Commercial Truck Wheel Alignment — Phase 1\n\n17. Air Brake Repair — Phase 1\n\n18. Electrical Diagnostics & Repair — Phase 1\n\n19. Diesel Truck A/C Repair — Phase 1\n\n20. Cooling Systems & Radiators — Phase 2\n\n21. Commercial Truck Tire Services — Phase 1\n\n22. Commercial Trailer Repair — Phase 1\n\n23. Trailer Welding & Structural Repairs — Phase 2\n\n24. Fifth-Wheel & Coupling Repair — Phase 2\n\n25. Preventive Maintenance & Routine Service — Phase 1\n\n26. Heavy Equipment Repair — Phase 2\n\n27. Agricultural Equipment Repair — Phase 2\n\n### Category C — Inspections & Compliance\n\n28. DOT Annual Inspections — Phase 1\n\n29. California 90-Day BIT Inspections — Phase 1\n\n30. Mobile Fleet Inspections — Phase 1\n\n31. CARB/Clean Truck Check Services — Phase 2, subject to verification of authorization\n\n32. Fleet Compliance & Inspection Reminders — Phase 2\n\n### Category D — Parts\n\n33. Commercial Truck Brake & Driveline Parts — Phase 1\n\n34. Engine, Filter & Maintenance Parts — Phase 2\n\n35. Parts Counter, Pickup & Delivery — Phase 2, subject to service confirmation\n\n### Category E — Customers & Industries\n\n36. Long-Haul Drivers & Dispatchers — Phase 1\n\n37. Local & Regional Commercial Fleets — Phase 1\n\n38. Agricultural Fleet Operators — Phase 2\n\n39. Construction & Heavy Equipment Operators — Phase 2\n\n40. Commercial Buses & Specialty Vehicles — Phase 2\n\n### Category F — Company Proof & Credibility\n\n41. Our Facility & Capabilities — Phase 1\n\n42. Heavy Lifting & Specialized Equipment — Phase 2\n\n43. Our Team & Technical Training — Phase 1\n\n44. Quality Control & Safety — Phase 1\n\n45. Customer Reviews & Repair Stories — Phase 1\n\n46. Apprenticeships & Careers — Phase 1\n\n### Category G — Resources & Service Coverage\n\n47. Service Area & I-5 / Highway 99 Access — Phase 1\n\n48. What Happens When You Call for Roadside Help — Phase 1\n\n49. Frequently Asked Questions — Phase 1\n\n50. Diesel Repair Education & Resources — Phase 1\n\n### Category H — Bonus Webpages\n\nThe following 10 additional substantive pages are expressly included at no additional charge. Their final subjects will be selected with Myers under Section 6.\n\n51. Bonus Page 01 — Phase 2 — Subject to Selection\n\n52. Bonus Page 02 — Phase 2 — Subject to Selection\n\n53. Bonus Page 03 — Phase 2 — Subject to Selection\n\n54. Bonus Page 04 — Phase 2 — Subject to Selection\n\n55. Bonus Page 05 — Phase 2 — Subject to Selection\n\n56. Bonus Page 06 — Phase 2 — Subject to Selection\n\n57. Bonus Page 07 — Phase 2 — Subject to Selection\n\n58. Bonus Page 08 — Phase 2 — Subject to Selection\n\n59. Bonus Page 09 — Phase 2 — Subject to Selection\n\n60. Bonus Page 10 — Phase 2 — Subject to Selection\n\nThese 10 pages carry no additional development fee and must be delivered as part of Phase 2.\n\n### Page Count Summary\n\n| Delivery | Pages |\n| --- | --- |\n| Phase 1 | 34 |\n| Phase 2 — Original Scope | 16 |\n| Phase 2 — Bonus Pages | 10 |\n| Total Contracted Pages | 60 |\n\n### Financial Summary\n\n| Item | Amount |\n| --- | --- |\n| Phase 1 | $17,000 |\n| Phase 2, including 10 bonus pages | $8,000 |\n| Total Project Investment | $25,000 |\n\n## Schedule B — Branding & Media Deliverables\n\nThe following are included within the $25,000 project price, subject to the detailed provisions of Sections 9 and 10.\n\n**Branding**\n\n- One approved modernized logo direction.\n- Existing Myers wordmark refinement/vectorization.\n- Up to three useful lockups.\n- Concise brand guide.\n- Favicon.\n- Open Graph image.\n- Agreed social avatars, banners, and profile graphics.\n- Google Business Profile image assets.\n\n**Facility Media**\n\n- One primary production session, up to eight hours.\n- One follow-up session, up to three hours if needed.\n- 30 delivered, edited photographs (at least 20 new on-site; up to 10 existing Myers-supplied if suitable).\n- One primary 45–60-second video.\n- Four shorter 10–30-second website video segments.\n- Approved aerial footage subject to lawful, safe operation.\n\nAdditional production beyond the agreed allowance requires written authorization.\n\n## Schedule C — Excluded and Future Services\n\nThe following services are not included in the fixed website price. They may be considered under separate written agreements:\n\n- Ongoing Search Everywhere Optimization.\n- Monthly SEO and GEO management.\n- Google Business Profile and Maps maintenance.\n- Managed customer-review programs.\n- Monthly content and video generation.\n- Google Ads management.\n- Google advertising spend.\n- Performance-based advertising commissions.\n- Meta Ads.\n- Social-media community management.\n- Custom business dashboards and repair-system integrations.\n- Managed Hosting & Care ($199/month) except if separately elected in Schedule D or included in another signed program.\n- New custom software or advanced AI automation.\n\nThe Parties have discussed a potential ongoing search/content program and performance-based advertising arrangement.\n\nThose discussions are nonbinding until separate agreements are executed.\n\nThere is no obligation to purchase those services as a condition of receiving the 60-page website or the 10 bonus pages.\n\n## Schedule D — Optional Managed Hosting & Care Election\n\n**This election is separate from the $25,000 website-development fee.** Catalyst provides complimentary project hosting during active development and for the 30-day post-completion/termination period stated in Section 17.2. To continue hosting under Catalyst's Vercel infrastructure afterward, Myers may elect the following service:\n\nManaged Hosting & Website Care — $199 per month\n\nIncludes Catalyst-managed commercial Vercel hosting, HTTPS/SSL, standard CDN delivery, routine availability/error monitoring, defined weekly source/content backups, daily database backups if persistent data is used, compatible routine security/dependency patches, and up to 60 minutes of minor website edits per month. See Section 17 for all limits, timing, termination and portability rights.\n\nMonth to month; billed in advance only after the complimentary hosting period ends. No yearly minimum. Included within a separately signed $4,000/month Catalyst search/content program while that program is active; not charged twice.\n\n**Choose one (neither option is preselected):**\n\n☐ **ACCEPT:** Myers authorizes the $199/month Managed Hosting & Care Plan after the complimentary hosting period and agrees to its terms in Section 17, with billing details to be separately set up using an authorized payment method.\n\n☐ **DECLINE:** Myers does not authorize a recurring hosting charge. Catalyst will provide the paid-for site and transition assistance under Section 17; Myers will arrange alternate hosting by the end of the complimentary period.\n\nIf neither is selected, the plan is deemed declined, and no recurring fee is authorized.\n\nHosting election confirmed by Myers's authorized signer (initials): \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_    Date: \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_. A change to this election may be made later through a separate written service order. A build kickoff Stripe payment does not constitute acceptance of this monthly plan.\n\n## Authorization and Signatures\n\nBy signing, the Parties acknowledge that they have reviewed and agree to the terms of this Agreement, including the 60-page project scope, payment milestones, branding and media deliverables, and the separate status of any future recurring services.\n\nService Provider\n\nCatalyst Digital Solutions, LLC\n\nAuthorized Representative\n\nMario Garza\n\nTitle\n\nAuthorized Representative\n\nSignature\n\nDate\n\nEmail\n\ninfo@catalystdigitalsolutions.com\n\nClient\n\nMyers Diesel Repair, Inc.\n\nDoing business as Myers Diesel Repair\n\nAuthorized Representative\n\nKenneth Wayne Myers\n\nTitle\n\nPresident\n\nSignature\n\nDate\n\nLegal Notices (to both)\n\nKen: myersdiesel@icloud.com  \nAbel: abelz@myersdiesel.com\n\nEnd of Agreement\n\nVersion 1.4 — November 13, 2026 · Draft for final business and legal approval before execution.";
